batch_limits:
Scope and method: all 6 repos were swept with sweep.py (all exited 0) into the sweep/<repo>.json files. Mirrors were reused or created only under oss-audit/mirrors. The only GitHub calls were the tool's cached GETs plus 2 rate_limit checks; nothing was pushed or changed. Core requests remaining at the end: 4531, shared with other auditors.

Extra static checks, run by my own scripts in oss-audit/auditor-rp6 (python -I, never executing repo code):
- a key=value / URL-password / const-password scan of HEAD and of every history blob;
- email classification by hash only, including a hash match against the owner's address;
- a shop-domain and Shopify GID census;
- a JWT payload-claims check, printing key names and exp only;
- a scan of the contents of all 29 saas zips and of the gzipped Playwright report;
- a look at 3 committed images (2 storefront banners, 1 production screenshot), extracted to scratch and then deleted.

Not done:
- No liveness test of any credential (no AWS, Stripe, Framer, DB or Vercel calls). Every 'looks live' is inferred.
- gitleaks does not decompress archives by default; I hand-scanned the archives, but other binaries (images) were not content-scanned.
- Commit identities are reported as counts only.
- refs/pull/* exist on the storefront (2) and rewardspro-production (3); I did not inspect them separately because a fresh snapshot excludes them.

Disclosure, my own handling slip: before I tightened my output masker, one tool output in this session printed an 18-character prefix of the storefront's historical AWS secret access key. It came from a truncated table inside the committed transcript, commit 0c41c90a76ae. Separately, a character-class shape (letters, digits and % positions, no actual characters) of the rewardspro-production DB password appeared in one output, along with the RDS dev-cluster hostname. Neither is in any file I wrote or in this result, but both are in this session's local transcript. Both credentials should be rotated anyway (see credentials_to_revoke). Rotation would also clear this.

Verified vs inferred: the secret classifications, email classes, licence files, identity counts and file locations are verified from the mirrors. The following are inferred: AI generation of the banners, the Shopify-template MIT origin, liveness of the AWS keys and DB passwords, and the 'superseded / no live deploy' claims, which rest on kingdom.yaml and README text.
